ll flex-col grow"><div data-message-author-role="user" data-message-id="bbb21424-fe8d-4ecf-85d6-0a9ed3f2977d" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden items-end rtl:items-start"><div class="relative max-w-[var(--user-chat-width,70%)] bg-token-message-surface rounded-3xl px-5 py-2.5"><div class="whitespace-pre-wrap">Title: Heave, Sway, and Surge: A Triaxial Theory of Constraint-Limited Cognition Target Journal: Behavioral and Brain Sciences Keywords: Embodied compression, cognitive bottlenecks, autoregressive generation, ecological constraints Abstract We propose a formal framework for modeling cognition as a triaxially constrained system. The theory posits three orthogonal axes: (1) Heaveâ€”compression of high-dimensional latent structures into discrete symbolic outputs; (2) Swayâ€”origination of constraints from anatomical, ecological, and architectural sources; and (3) Surgeâ€”time-binding dynamics that transform latent trajectories into sequential behavior. By unifying embodied cognitive science, ecological psychology, and large language model (LLM) architecture, this model formalizes the structure-function relationship in both biological and artificial systems. We provide mathematical formalizations for each axis, derive empirically testable predictions, and demonstrate cognitive homologies between animals, humans, and LLMs. This approach reframes symbolic behavior (e.g., language) not as evidence of planning or abstraction, but as the emergent output of constraint satisfaction within a lossy representational manifold. 1. Introduction Contemporary models of cognition often treat language, planning, and problem-solving as the product of symbolic manipulation or representational foresight. Such views struggle to account for the embodied, ecological, and architectural constraints that shape actual behaviorâ€”whether in biological organisms or artificial systems. We propose a unifying framework that treats cognition as an emergent product of constraint-limited traversal through high-dimensional latent spaces. Rather than assuming internal scripts or plans, we model language and behavior as products of three interacting forces: Heave: the compression of continuous latent dynamics into discrete, expressive outputs, Sway: the origin of constraints, whether anatomical (e.g., vocal tract geometry), ecological (e.g., terrain, affordances), or architectural (e.g., context window size), Surge: the irreversible binding of latent transitions into time-sequenced outputs. We argue that this triaxial model offers a common language for understanding beaver dam construction, human speech articulation, and autoregressive text generation in transformers. Across all systems, cognition emerges not from abstract planning, but from structure-preserving reductions conditioned by environment and embodiment. In the sections that follow, we formalize each axis mathematically, relate it to neural and computational architectures, and derive testable predictions across cognitive science, ethology, and machine learning. 2. Axis I: Compression (Heave) 2.1 Conceptual Overview Cognition begins in a continuous, high-dimensional representational space, where perceptual, sensorimotor, or abstract information resides as latent trajectories. These trajectories are not directly communicable; they must be compressed into discrete outputs that are legible to other systems. In biological organisms, this process is constrained by the articulatory mechanics of the vocal tract or gestural systems. In artificial agents, it is constrained by vocabulary tokenization schemes. We refer to this transformation as Heave: the act of lifting latent structure into compressible form. 2.2 Formal Definition Let&nbsp; denote the latent representational space and&nbsp; denote the discrete token space. The compression function is defined as: f_C: \mathcal{V} \rightarrow \mathcal{T}, \quad f_C(\mathbf{v}) = \arg\max_{t_i \in \mathcal{T}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{e}_{t_i} \rangle The mapping is subject to projection constraints: For humans: , encoding physiological limits (e.g., tongue curvature, breath control). For LLMs: , reflecting vocabulary and subword encoding heuristics. 2.3 Empirical Examples In speech production, the articulatory system limits expressivity to a small, culturally defined phoneme set. Vowel formant distributions provide a canonical example of lossy projectionâ€”only a narrow region of acoustic space can be stably produced. In LLMs, tokenization schemes (e.g., byte-pair encoding) similarly constrain what concepts can be compactly represented, influencing everything from compositional depth to inference tractability. Compression thus serves both as an expressive bottleneck and a formal necessity: without it, no systemâ€”natural or artificialâ€”can produce symbolic outputs at scale. Heave, in this view, is not an abstraction layer but a dimensional reduction operator, projecting cognition into action or speech under external constraints. 3. Axis II: Constraint Origin (Sway) 3.1 Taxonomy of Constraints All cognitive systems operate within a set of structural limitations that restrict the trajectories available within their latent spaces. We categorize these constraints into three functional classes: : Anatomical constraints derived from biophysical structures (e.g., working memory capacity, vocal tract morphology). : Ecological constraints arising from interaction with physical and social environments (e.g., terrain geometry, visual occlusion, social signaling density). : Architectural constraints imposed by the information processing framework itself (e.g., attention window size, recurrence limits, or token capacity). 3.2 Formal Space Restriction We define the set of admissible cognitive states as: This represents a bounded submanifold within , shaped by multiple concurrent sources of constraint. 3.3 Comparative Examples In animal behavior, squirrels caching food do not plan long-term; they act opportunistically, guided by terrain features and olfactory cuesâ€”examples of . Similarly, beavers do not blueprint their dams. Instead, logs accumulate via hydrodynamic affordances, while dam completion is driven by sensitivity to water soundâ€”an emergent behavioral constraint. In LLMs, is instantiated in the fixed context window and in parameter-specific architectural bottlenecks. These constraints define how much prior context can be used to generate a token, thereby influencing reasoning complexity and depth. Constraint-origin thus determines the shape of cognitionâ€”what actions or utterances are even possibleâ€”before any decision process or learning occurs. 4. Axis III: Temporal Binding (Surge) 4.1 Dynamic State Transition Cognition unfolds across time through an irreversible process of sequential activation. In both biological and artificial systems, each expressive act constrains the next, enforcing a directional logic. We formalize this with an autoregressive update rule: \mathbf{v}_{t+1} = F(\mathbf{v}_{\leq t}, u_t), \quad y_{t+1} = f_C(\mathbf{v}_{t+1})  4.2 Directionality and Irreversibility This process is inherently non-reversible: once a token is produced, it modifies the context space for subsequent updates. In humans, this corresponds to speech articulation as a one-way gateâ€”each phoneme restricts future syntactic possibilities. In LLMs, the causal masking in transformer layers enforces strict autoregression. This gives rise to what we term the Token Event Horizon: the boundary where a latent representational field must be collapsed into a discrete emission. Once crossed, this act is entangled with memory and coherence, and cannot be retracted without structural discontinuity. Surge thus encodes the temporally grounded irreversibility of symbolic behavior. It accounts for the seriality of speech, action, and thoughtâ€”and highlights how coherence is maintained not by foresight, but by local constraint propagation across time. 5. Theoretical Integration 5.1 Comparison with Global Workspace Theory Our model contrasts with Global Workspace Theory (GWT), which frames cognition in terms of central access and broadcast mechanisms. GWT assumes a core workspace where pre-conscious representations are integrated for global dissemination. In contrast, our triaxial framework avoids centralized representation. Constraint-limited cognition arises from the projection, restriction, and sequentialization of local latent states, making no assumptions about workspace unification or executive access. 5.2 Relation to Active Inference Our framework aligns structurally with Active Inference, which models agents as minimizing variational free energy over time. However, while Active Inference emphasizes the optimization of belief states, we emphasize the emergent shaping of action and expression through externally imposed constraints. Surge and Heave in particular can be seen as orthogonal to action-perception loops: they are formatting operations that prepare latent content for emission, rather than internal predictive updates. 5.3 Bridging Embodiment and Computation Constraint-limited cognition offers a formal pathway for unifying embodied and computational models. It treats the act of speaking, building, or generating tokens not as a function of abstract reasoning, but as the product of latent state compression under anatomical, ecological, or architectural constraints. Whether in cortex or in transformer architecture, the same logic applies: cognition is shaped by what can be compressed, given structural limitations, and emitted in time. 6. Predictions and Experiments The triaxial model of constraint-limited cognition produces a range of empirically testable predictions across neurobiology, ethology, and machine learning. Below, we outline three primary predictions along with suggested experimental frameworks. 6.1 Phonological Bottlenecks and Semantic Boundaries Prediction: Languages with tighter articulatory constraints (e.g., fewer vowels or consonant clusters) will exhibit coarser semantic partitioning in lexical categories. Rationale: The projection from latent space to phonological space (Heave) compresses meaning resolution. Tighter constraints yield greater semantic overlap per token. Test: Cross-linguistic corpus analysis of formant density vs. lexical ambiguity; acoustic-articulatory modeling of vowel formant constraints. 6.2 Gesture-Token Tradeoff Prediction: Languages with lower phonological expressivity will compensate with more elaborate co-speech gesture systems. Rationale: When the phonological channel is under-constrained, meaning offloads into the gestural domainâ€”a rebalancing of compression under Sway. Test: Motion-capture studies across linguistic populations with varying phoneme inventories; multimodal LLM training to assess gesture-latent alignment. 6.3 Nonlinear Context Window Effects in LLMs Prediction: Expanding transformer context windows yields diminishing returns in multistep reasoning tasks, with performance asymptoting due to architectural constraints (Sway) and tokenization granularity (Heave). Rationale: Constraint-origin and compression jointly limit reasoning depth, irrespective of surface context length. Test: Evaluation of LLMs on compositional reasoning benchmarks under increasing context window sizes; analysis of latent path divergence and entropy as a function of window length. These predictions illustrate how our model guides both foundational theory and empirical methodology across disciplines. They also suggest practical implications for LLM design, embodied learning models, and cross-species behavioral inference. 7. Figures Figure 1. The Constraint Cube A 3D schematic illustrating the three orthogonal axes: X-axis (Heave): Compression from latent to token space Y-axis (Sway): Gradient of constraint origin (anatomical to architectural) Z-axis (Surge): Temporal progression from latent dynamics to output sequence Annotations include examples at extreme points: squirrel caching, human phonology, and transformer softmax. Figure 2. The Token Event Horizon A comparative diagram of: Panel A: Human spectrogram showing formant trajectories limited by articulatory constraints Panel B: LLM logit output distribution before and after softmax collapse, illustrating loss of latent complexity Figure 3. Constraint-Deformed Vector Fields Visualization of latent trajectory deformation under distinct constraint types: Subfigure 3a: Anatomical gating (e.g., reduced working memory dimensionality) Subfigure 3b: Ecological filtering (e.g., dam completion via flow-guided perception) Subfigure 3c: Transformer architectural distortion (e.g., attention head sparsity) 8. Conclusion We have proposed a unified triaxial frameworkâ€”Heave, Sway, and Surgeâ€”to model cognition across biological and artificial systems. By formalizing the processes of latent compression, constraint shaping, and temporal binding, this theory challenges conventional assumptions about symbolic planning and internal representation. Instead, it posits that coherent cognitive outputs emerge through constraint-limited traversal of high-dimensional representational spaces. Our formulation integrates insights from embodied cognition, ecological psychology, neuroscience, and AI architecture. It recasts behaviorâ€”not as pre-planned computationâ€”but as the satisficing product of externally shaped internal dynamics. The model provides mathematically explicit mechanisms to explain token generation, gesture-behavior coupling, planning illusions, and reasoning asymptotes. Future directions include experimental validation in cross-linguistic speech analysis, ecological robotics, and comparative LLM benchmarks. Ultimately, this work offers a blueprint for reconceptualizing cognition as emergent structure rather than symbolic deliberationâ€”a shift with broad implications for the science of mind, machine learning, and neurocomputational theory. Appendix: Mathematical Formalization A.1 Latent Space and Tokenization Let represent a continuous latent space. Define a discrete token space , with each token associated with embedding . Define the compression function: f_C: \mathcal{V} \rightarrow \mathcal{T}, \quad f_C(\mathbf{v}) = \arg\max_{t_i \in \mathcal{T}} \langle \mathbf{v}, \mathbf{e}_{t_i} \rangle  Subject to projection constraints: \phi_{\text{articulatory}} \subset \mathbb{R}^n, \quad \phi_{\text{tokenizer}} \subset \mathbb{R}^n  A.2 Constraint Manifold Let be subsets representing valid trajectories under anatomical, ecological, and architectural constraints, respectively. Define the admissible state manifold: \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{V} \cap (\Gamma_{\text{anat}} \cup \Gamma_{\text{eco}} \cup \Gamma_{\text{arch}})  This defines a composite bounded region within latent space where cognition is operable. A.3 Temporal Transition and Output Define time-indexed latent states , influenced by preceding history and external inputs: \mathbf{v}_{t+1} = F(\mathbf{v}_{\leq t}, u_t), \quad y_{t+1} = f_C(\mathbf{v}_{t+1})  Let be external or recurrent contextual inputs. The mapping defines transition dynamics (e.g., cortical integration or transformer layer outputs). A.4 Token Event Horizon Define a collapse operation such that: C(\mathbf{v}) = \text{argmax}_i \ p(t_i | \mathbf{v})  This operator models the irreversible reduction of latent probability distributions into symbolic emissionsâ€”biologically via speech articulation, computationally via sampling from softmax logits. References Andreas, J. (2023). Language models as agent models. Proceedings of ACL 2023. Arbib, M. A. (2005). From monkey-like action recognition to human language: An evolutionary framework for neurolinguistics. Behavioral and Brain Sciences, 28(2), 105â€“124. Baddeley, A. D. (2003). Working memory and language: An overview. Journal of 